EXT. FLOODED RICE TERRACE - DAWN

Mist hangs over stepped paddies like breath on glass. Water mirrors a pink sky. Dragonflies skim the surface.

Two soldiers wade along a bund, rifles held high, boots sucking in mud. MIRO KOVACEVIC (30s, stubble, helmet strapped crooked) leads. ELIAS NORDQUIST (20s, round-faced, a rosary wound around his wrist) follows two steps behind.

Silence. Frogs. A distant temple bell.

He gestures. They stop. He listens. The frogs go quiet at once, all together, in a sudden, hollow hush.

                    ELIAS
          (whisper)
          Did you hear that?

                    MIRO
          Hear what?

                    ELIAS
          Nothing. That's what I mean.

Miro nods, lowers himself to one knee, sets his rifle across it. Elias does the same, a beat late, splashing. They both flinch.

He scans the far terrace. Smoke from a thatched hut, thin and lazy. A water buffalo standing in the mud with its head down. Nothing wrong. Nothing in the world wrong.

                    MIRO
          Wait here.

                    ELIAS
          Where are you going?

                    MIRO
          To look at the buffalo.

                    ELIAS
          It's just a buffalo.

                    MIRO
          Then it won't mind.

He wades forward. Elias's eyes dart left, right, back to the hut. A wooden door creaks open on its own, and a tin bucket tips off the step; water flows across the planks.

Both soldiers freeze. Then the buffalo lifts its head, and behind it, in the mud, rise rifle barrels, one after another, like stalks of rice in the dawn.

Miro lunges back and grabs Elias by the collar, dragging him down behind the bund as the first shot cracks. Mud sprays. Elias fires blindly; Miro slaps the muzzle skyward, shouting, and they roll together along the slope, each pulling the other, each pushing, until they slam against a stone marker half-buried in the dyke and lie there panting, rifles tangled between them.

                    ELIAS
          You said it was clear!

                    MIRO
          I said it looked clear.

                    ELIAS
          That's not the same!

                    MIRO
          That's what I'm saying.

Another volley whines overhead. Elias presses his face into the mud. Miro, breathing hard, reaches over and squeezes his shoulder.

                    MIRO (CONT'D)
          Count with me.

                    ELIAS
          To what?

                    MIRO
          To three.

They count, silently, lips moving, then rise as one.
